course: dictionary
lesson: 11741
title: Backache
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Backache is a noun. It means a pain in your back. «Mia had a backache after carrying her heavy school bag all day.» The clues are heavy bag and all day, so the pain is in her back. Backache is that pain.
explain: Sitting badly can also cause backache. «Tom slumped on the sofa for hours and woke up with backache.» Slumped means sitting with your back bent and loose, so the clue tells you why his back hurt.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. Put in «a pain in your back» for backache: «Tom woke up with a pain in his back.» It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Backache is a noun, and its plural is backaches.
fact: «Backache» means a pain in your back.
fact: Carrying a heavy school bag or sitting badly can give you backache.
fact: In the UK people say «have backache»; in the US people say «have a backache».
fact: A good check is to ask if the sentence is about your back hurting. If it is, backache fits.
exercise: «After carrying his heavy bag up the hill, Sam had backache.» What is backache? | A bag for carrying books | A long walk up a hill | *A pain in your back | A hot drink
exercise: «Lily sat hunched over her desk all afternoon and got backache.» What caused her backache? | Singing loudly | Swimming in the sea | Eating too much cake | *Sitting badly
exercise: Find and copy the word that means a pain in your back. | *backache | school | heavy | carry
test: «Dad said his backache came from sitting in the car all day.» What does backache mean? | A new car | A school trip | *A pain in your back | A cold drink
test: Which is the correct plural of backache? | backache's | backachies | *backaches | backachs
exam: Mia says «I have a backache» and Tom says «I have backache». Who is right? | Only Mia is right, because backache needs a | Neither, because backache means a pain in your leg | Only Tom is right, because backache never has a | *Both are correct: one is the US way and one is the UK way
